<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pytheas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 325 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Pytheas of Massalia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pytheas was a Greek navigator and geographer from Massalia, modern Marseille, who made a voyage to north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> around 325 BCE. His work On the Ocean is lost but was quoted by later writers. He described <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> trade, the tides, and a distant northern land he called Thule, giving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> world its first detailed account of these regions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pytheas.jpg" alt="Pytheas" width="320" height="135" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mellangoose · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parisinus_gr_1397_f.162_pytheas_of_massalia_from_Strabo_7.3.1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pytheas came from the Greek colony of Massalia, modern Marseille, and worked as a geographer, explorer and astronomer. His own account of his voyage did not survive. Only quotations and summaries remain in later writers, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>'s Geographica, Diodorus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and Pliny's Natural History. Strabo tried to discredit him. Dicaearchus, who died about 285 BCE, distrusted his stories, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephorus">Ephorus</a> do not mention him, which helps to date the voyage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Pytheas made his voyage to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in about 325 BCE and was the first documented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> mariner to reach the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a>. Where it began is unknown. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> had closed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-gibraltar">Strait of Gibraltar</a> to ships of other nations, so some historians think he went overland to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a>, while others think he hugged the coast. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> reports that he travelled over the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> that was accessible. He called the island Prettanike, the earliest known form of the name Britain, and named its corners Kantion, Belerion and Orkas, taken to be Kent, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>, Diodorus says, people mined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>, smelted it and shaped it into pieces like knucklebones, which were carried at low tide to the island of Ictis and then on horses for 30 days to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a>. Pytheas also described Thule, and a frozen sea one day's sail beyond it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Pytheas was the first known Greek to describe the Arctic, polar ice and the Celtic and Germanic tribes, and the first on record to describe the midnight sun. He measured latitudes with the gnomon, and his account of the tides is the earliest known to suggest that the moon causes them. Pliny reports his claim that the tide in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> rose 80 cubits, far beyond any known British tide. Dicaearchus, Timaeus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eratosthenes">Eratosthenes</a>, Hipparchus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, Artemidorus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/posidonius">Posidonius</a> are among the ancient authors known to have seen his text, so he was a central source for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> and the subarctic lands of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pytheas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Greeks from Phocaea founded Massalia about 600 BCE, and Greek wine, bronze vessels and painted pottery travelled up the Rhône to the Hallstatt princes, while Etruscan beaked flagons crossed the Alps to the first La Tène nobles; about 325 BCE <b>Pytheas of Massalia</b> sailed to Britain and beyond.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Greek writers from <b>Pytheas</b> onwards describe the tin trade of the far south-west, and harbours such as Mount Batten on Plymouth Sound linked the region with Brittany and the wider Atlantic world.</blockquote><a href="/state/dumnonii">Dumnonii</a></li><li><blockquote>Artemis of Ephesus and Apollo Delphinios were its chief cults, and around 325 BCE its navigator <b>Pytheas</b> sailed to Britain and the North Sea.</blockquote><a href="/state/massalia">Massalia</a></li><li><blockquote>The Greek explorer <b>Pytheas</b> probably referred to them as the Ostimioi around 320 BCE, placing them on a promontory facing the Ocean.</blockquote><a href="/state/osismii">Osismii</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Massalia, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.3,5.37&amp;year=-325" aria-label="Massalia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4679 628 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pytheas"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5129 898h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5129 898h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.3,5.37&amp;year=-325">What was here in 325 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
